-- of a sign Q (x ≤ 0, or x ≥ 0) with its strict form Qs: Q 0; Q closed under sums; c²·d has the sign of d; a sum of two
-- of one sign that is 0 has both 0; c²·d = 0 with d strict gives c = 0
sy-QL : (A : Type) → A → (A → A → A) → (A → A → A) → (A → Type) → (A → Type) → Type
sy-QL A z ad mu Q Qs = Q z × (((x y : A) → Q x → Q y → Q (ad x y)) × (((c d : A) → Qs d → Q (mu (mu c c) d)) × (((x y : A) → Q x → Q y → ad x y ≡ z → x ≡ z) × (((x y : A) → Q x → Q y → ad x y ≡ z → y ≡ z) × ((c d : A) → Qs d → mu (mu c c) d ≡ z → c ≡ z)))))
